---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Why I started LightPhotos — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A photo shoot with friends, a Lightroom license that stopped working, and an afternoon spent building the small photo editor I actually needed."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blog</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-06-23">June 23, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Why I started LightPhotos</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A Lightroom license that stopped working, a few apps that didn't fit,
          and an afternoon spent building the small photo editor I actually needed.
        </p>
      </header>

      <h2>A folder full of RAW files</h2>
      <p>
        I recently went on a photo shoot with some friends. I brought my Sony
        full-frame camera and shot RAW, as I usually do. It was a good
        afternoon, and I came home with a memory card full of photos they were
        waiting to see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I copied the files to my Mac. Straight out of the camera, RAW
        files aren't finished pictures. They look flat, some are a stop too
        dark, and the highlights and shadows hold detail you only see after you
        pull them back. Every photo needed some adjustment before I would share
        it. I didn't want to send my friends a zip of unedited ARW files and
        call it done.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I needed a photo editor, and I found that I didn't have one that
        worked.
      </p>

      <h2>The tools I already had</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Lightroom Classic.</strong> Years ago I paid close to $200 for a
        perpetual Lightroom Classic license. That was the point of paying up
        front: buy it once and keep it. It stopped being true when I upgraded
        macOS. Starting around Monterey, the installer won't run on current
        versions of the OS. The license is still mine, and I have no way to use
        it.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Luminar AI.</strong> I had also bought a copy of Luminar AI. It
        was OK. The version I had was slow and crashed often, so I spent more
        time waiting on it and restarting it than editing. When I want to fix
        the exposure on forty photos, that gets old quickly.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Apple Photos.</strong> Apple Photos comes with every Mac, and I
        like what it can do. But it had several deal breakers for me.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first is the library format. The whole library is a single file on
        disk, in a proprietary format that only Apple Photos can read. You
        can't just browse your photos as ordinary files in ordinary folders.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second is that I have already been hurt by that format. I lost several years of photos
        because a newer version of macOS refused to open Photos libraries from
        my older backups. The backups were there and the files were intact,
        but the only program that could read them wouldn't. I don't want to
        trust my photos to a format like that again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third is storage. I keep my main drive for recent work,
        and older photos go on other drives. I don't want hundreds of gigabytes
        of old shoots on my main drive, locked inside a library that I might
        not be able to open in a few years.
      </p>

      <h2>The free alternatives</h2>
      <p>
        Next I looked at the free options, starting with Darktable. It is
        capable software and clearly the work of people who care a lot about
        photography. But opening it felt like sitting in the cockpit of a plane
        I wasn't qualified to fly. There are modules on top of modules, many
        ways to do the same thing, and a large vocabulary to learn before you
        can make a picture a little brighter.
      </p>
      <p>
        The other free tools I tried had the same problem in different amounts.
        They had a lot of features I didn't want and an interface I would have
        to study. I wasn't trying to learn a new craft. I wanted to finish a
        job I already knew how to do.
      </p>

      <h2>$10 a month</h2>
      <p>
        That left the obvious answer: pay Adobe for Lightroom again. The
        photography plan costs about $10 a month. That isn't a lot of money,
        and I know plenty of photographers get far more than $10 of value from
        it every month.
      </p>
      <p>
        I'm not one of them. I edit photos in bursts: a trip, an event, an
        afternoon with friends. Then weeks or months can pass before I open an
        editor again. A subscription charges me the same amount in the months I
        don't use it, and it would have been the second time I paid for the
        same software. I had already paid for a license I can no longer run, so
        renting the same thing felt wrong.
      </p>

      <h2>So I built one</h2>
      <p>
        That afternoon, I sat down to build a basic version myself. I didn't
        want to clone Lightroom. I wanted the smallest tool that would get these
        photos edited and into my friends' hands. I wrote down what it had to
        do:
      </p>
      <ol class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Open any folder and show the photos in it.</strong> No
          catalog, no import step, no library file. My photos are already
          organized in plain folders on whatever drive holds them, and I want
          to point the app at one and start working. If the app goes away,
          the photos are still ordinary files I can open with anything.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Rate photos from 1 to 5.</strong> After a shoot, most of the
          work is culling. I need to go through a few hundred frames fast and
          mark the ones worth keeping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Filter by rating.</strong> After I rate them, I want to see
          only the 4s and 5s and ignore everything else.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Adjust exposure, highlights, shadows and the other basics.</strong>
          Those few sliders handle most of what a RAW file needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Keep every adjustment non-destructive.</strong> The original
          file is never modified. Edits are saved separately, so I can change
          my mind, reset a photo, or come back to it months later.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Export selected photos to JPEG.</strong> The whole point was
          to get shareable files out the other end.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Run on a Mac.</strong> That was the only machine I needed it
          on.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Anything that wasn't on the list didn't make it into the first version,
        on purpose. The list is short because the problem was small: a folder
        of RAW files, a few friends waiting, and no editor I could use.
      </p>

      <h2>What comes next</h2>
      <p>
        Even a small tool like this involves a lot of decisions: how to decode
        RAW files, where the adjustment math runs, how edits are stored next to
        the originals, and what to build it with. Some of those decisions paid
        off, and others came with real costs.
      </p>
      <p>
        In a future post, I'll go through the architectural choices behind
        LightPhotos and the pros and cons of each.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
